 \ReviewerComment{7. The Q95 threshold of 0.5740 mm may not represent meteorologically significant heavy rainfall. Please justify this threshold and clarify the influence of spatial averaging on extreme precipitation.}
 
-\PendingResponse
-
-\ChangeLocation
+\AuthorResponse{We agree that this numerical threshold should not be interpreted as a meteorologically defined heavy-rainfall intensity. Q95 is a training-derived empirical 95th-percentile cutoff used to assess the relative upper tail of the regional forecast target, held fixed for the reported test diagnostics. We report it as 0.5740~mm\,h$^{-1}$ because the target is the hourly-mean rate for a single hour; it is not a six-hour precipitation total. The existing percentile terminology and WMO time-scale/spatial-support qualification are retained.
+
+No logarithmic transformation was used to define Q95. We first form the equal-weight mean of the 25 cells at each time, then take its percentile over the 18,404 training prediction-target times; this is not an average of cell quantiles. On these identical times, regional Q95 is 0.574048~mm\,h$^{-1}$, versus a cell range of 0.5441--0.6990 and a median of 0.618935~mm\,h$^{-1}$. Thus the regional Q95 is 7.3\% below the cell median while still lying within the cell range. We do not attribute its low absolute magnitude solely to spatial averaging.
+
+The distributional contrasts are not uniform. Regional wet-hour frequency and Q90 are 14.3\% and 8.8\% above their respective cell medians. Q99, Q99.9 and the maximum are 13.1\%, 13.9\% and 31.4\% below the corresponding medians and are each below all 25 cells. These are descriptive statistics on matched training times, not significance statements or estimates of a causal mechanism. Each maximum belongs to its own series and need not occur at a common event time.
+
+We added Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m7_spatial_quantiles} with all 25 cells and the regional mean, and the author-confirmed Supplementary Figure~\ref{supp-sfig:r2m7_spatial_quantiles}. The figure normalizes each of Q90/Q95/Q99/Q99.9/Max by the median of that same statistic across the 25 cells; the dashed ratio-one line marks the corresponding median. The table, figure and text use the same 18,404 targets, including all maxima, avoiding mixed statistical periods. Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:experimental_setup} explains the threshold construction, Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:regime_results} reports the comparison, and the Discussion and Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:limitations} qualify spatial smoothing and transfer to station-level extremes. A gridded ERA5 cell is not a gauge, and these contrasts do not establish station-scale extreme-rainfall skill.}
+
+\ManuscriptChange{Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:experimental_setup} (threshold construction), Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:regime_results} (spatial statistics), Discussion and Section~\ref{manuscript-sec:limitations}; Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m7_spatial_quantiles} and Figure~\ref{supp-sfig:r2m7_spatial_quantiles}. Final page and line numbers pending.}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Training-derived Q95 on the original precipitation scale}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{For the spatial-support diagnostic, all 25 grid-cell series and their equal-weight regional mean use the same 18,404 training prediction-target times. We first form the regional mean at each time and then take its empirical 95th percentile, rather than averaging grid-cell quantiles. This gives Q95 $=0.574048$~mm\,h$^{-1}$ on the original precipitation scale, without a logarithmic transformation. Individual-cell Q95 values range from 0.5441 to 0.6990~mm\,h$^{-1}$. Q95 defines a fixed relative upper-tail regime of the regional hourly target, not a meteorological heavy-rainfall category.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Distribution-dependent spatial contrasts}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{A separate descriptive spatial comparison uses the training targets, rather than the forecast-test samples. Relative to the median of the corresponding statistic across the 25 cells, regional wet-hour frequency ($\geq0.1$~mm\,h$^{-1}$) is 14.3\% higher and Q90 is 8.8\% higher. Regional Q95 is 7.3\% lower than the cell median but remains within the individual-cell range. Regional Q99, Q99.9 and the maximum are lower by 13.1\%, 13.9\% and 31.4\%, respectively, and all three are below the corresponding values of every one of the 25 cells. These percentages are relative changes, not percentage-point differences.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Matched-time supplementary table and per-statistic normalization}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{Supplementary Table~\ref{supp-stab:r2m7_spatial_quantiles} lists the 26 series on the common training-time set. Supplementary Figure~\ref{supp-sfig:r2m7_spatial_quantiles} divides each displayed statistic by the median of that same statistic across the 25 cells. Its five categories therefore have separate normalizing denominators, with a reference ratio of one.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Descriptive interpretation of spatial averaging}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{The spatial contrasts depend on the position in the precipitation distribution: wet frequency and Q90 increase relative to the cell medians, while the higher quantiles and maxima decrease. Spatial averaging should therefore not be described as reducing every precipitation intensity. These are descriptive contrasts between matched-time series summaries; the individual maxima need not occur at the same time, and the comparison does not identify an event-scale causal mechanism.}
+}
+
+\EvidenceBox{Quantitative spatial-support limitation}{
+\RevisedExcerpt{On the common training-target set, the regional Q99, Q99.9 and maximum are 13.1\%, 13.9\% and 31.4\% below their respective 25-cell medians. The opposite-direction contrasts in wet frequency and Q90 qualify a blanket smoothing interpretation. A single ERA5 cell is itself a gridded estimate rather than a rain-gauge measurement, so these grid-to-region comparisons do not quantify station-level extreme-rainfall attenuation. The conclusions concern ERA5-derived regional-mean hourly precipitation and do not directly represent station-scale extremes.}
+}
 
