 Several limitations affect the interpretation and generalization of the present results. The first concerns the spatial representation of precipitation. All meteorological variables, including the prediction target, were spatially averaged across the 25 ERA5 grid points within the study domain. The resulting forecasts therefore represent Dublin regional mean precipitation rather than rainfall at individual locations. \textcolor{red}{Spatial averaging provides a compact regional forecasting problem but can smooth localized rainfall maxima and reduce the apparent intensity of small-scale precipitation events. Consequently, upper-tail precipitation events ($\geq$Q95) of the ERA5 regional mean must not be equated with heavy rainfall measured at an individual station. This distinction limits the interpretation of the upper-tail results as local high-impact rainfall forecasts.}
+{\color{red}\hypersetup{linkcolor=red,citecolor=red,filecolor=red,urlcolor=red}
